{"text":"{
  "status": "STABLE",
  "core_findings": [
    "Masked-target separation is positive across all four categories. Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics are weaker: negatives outrank positives at 8.01% and 6.65% of probed positions, versus 3.97% for Movies_and_TV and 4.59% for CDs_and_Vinyl.",
    "Representations retain substantial variation rather than showing complete collapse. Appended-MASK user representations occupy fewer effective dimensions than item embeddings, especially in Industrial_and_Scientific."
  ],
  "key_implications": [
    "The model separates most measured training positives from sampled negatives; these probes do not establish validation ranking quality.",
    "User representations are more concentrated than item representations, but directional alignment alone does not establish collapse or recommendation diversity.",
    "Diagnostic terminal-position coverage near 19–20% does not identify training branch allocation."
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "headline_metrics": {
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:pos_neg_margin_p10": 0.3545,
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": 0.4825,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:real_items_rankme": 48.7496,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:appended_mask_users_rankme": 17.339
    },
    "brief_metric_read": {
      "pos_neg_margin_p10": "Lower-tail separation is positive but narrow in the two weaker categories.",
      "representation_geometry": "Item RankMe spans 48.75–50.83; user RankMe spans 17.34–28.74, indicating greater user concentration."
    }
  }
}"}
